# DESCRIPTION
# A problem that asks students to find probability values on the normal curve.
#
# This problem is derived from a homework problem in Introductory Statistics,
# licensed by OpenStax under a Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY 4.0).
# Modified for WeBWorK by Michael Stassen   mstassen(at)fitchburgstate(dot)edu
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Probability)
## DBchapter(Continuous distributions)
## DBsection(Gaussian normal)
## KEYWORDS('normal distribution','percentile', 'probability')
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Michael Stassen)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',     "PGML.pl",
    "contextPercent.pl", "PGstatisticsmacros.pl",
);

Context("Numeric");

$mu    = 250;
$sigma = 50;

$a  = 205;
$Za = ($a - $mu) / $sigma;
$Pa = normal_prob(-'infty', $Za);

$b  = 285;
$Zb = ($b - $mu) / $sigma;
$Pb = normal_prob($Zb, 'infty');

$c  = 295;
$Zc = ($c - $mu) / $sigma;
$Pc = normal_prob(-'infty', $Zc);

$d  = 345;
$Zd = ($d - $mu) / $sigma;
$Pd = normal_prob(-'infty', $Zd);

$Pcd = $Pd - $Pc;

$k  = 90;
$Zk = normal_distr($k / 100 - .5);
$Xk = $mu + $sigma * $Zk;

$m  = 70;
$Zm = normal_distr($m / 200);
$X1 = $mu - $sigma * $Zm;
$X2 = $mu + $sigma * $Zm;

Context("Percent");
Context()->flags->set(
    decimalPlaces => 2,
    tolerance     => .05,
);

BEGIN_PGML
Suppose that the distance of fly balls hit to the outfield (in baseball) is
normally distributed with a mean of [$mu] feet and a standard deviation of [$sigma]
feet.

a. If one fly ball is randomly chosen from this distribution, what is the
probability that this ball traveled less than [$a] feet?.[____]{$Pa}
b. If one fly ball is randomly chosen from this distribution, what is the
probability that this ball traveled more than [$b] feet?.[____]{$Pb}
c. If one fly ball is randomly chosen from this distribution, what is the
probability that this ball traveled between [$c] and [$d] feet?.[____]{$Pcd}
d. The [$k]th percentile of the distribution of fly balls is [____]{$Xk} feet.
e. The middle [$m]% of fly ball distances fall between [____]{$X1} and [____]{$X2} feet.

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
In all of these cases, the normal probability distribution is [` N([$mu],[$sigma])`]:
The result can be looked up with software or by converting to the standard normal
and using a table. 
a. Find [`P(X < [$a]) = [$Pa]`]
b. Find [`P(X > [$b]) = [$Pb]`]
c. Find [`P([$c] < X < [$c]) = [$Pcd] `]
d. One needs the value [`a`] such that  [`P(Z < a) = 0.9 `] on the standard
normal curve.  This can be found to be [`a=[$Zk]`] and then converting to the given
distribution with
[``` x = \mu + a \sigma = [$mu] + [$Zk] \cdot [$sigma] = [$Xk] ```]
e. First, one needs to find the value of [`a`] such that [` P(-a < Z< a = [$m/100])`] on 
the standard normal curve.  This can be done easier knowing that there is [@ 1-$m/100 @] in the two tails and thus [@ (1-$m/100)*0.5 @] in one tail so that 
[``` P(-a < Z < a) = [$m/100]```]
is true for the same value of [`a`] as  
[``` P(Z<a) = [@ $m + (100-$m)/2 @] ```]
This value can be looked up in a table or using software to get
[``` a = [$Zm] ```]
and then converted to the given normal with 
[``` 
\begin{aligned}
x_1 & = \mu - a \sigma = [$mu] - [$Zm] \cdot [$sigma] = [$X1] \\
x_2 & = \mu + a \sigma = [$mu] + [$Zm] \cdot [$sigma] = [$X2] 
\end{aligned}
```]
Therefore the middle [$m] will fall between [$X1] feet and [$X2] feet.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
